Two systems, written by two people the same afternoon, doing comparable work over comparable entity counts. One runs in 0.2 ms. The other runs in 2.4 ms. Same query shape, same math, same allocator. No errors, no warnings, no logs.

The only difference in the source is where an attribute is:

    // fast one
    [BurstCompile]
    public partial struct A : ISystem {
        [BurstCompile] public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState s) { }
    }

    // slow one
    public partial struct B : ISystem {
        [BurstCompile] public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState s) { }
    }

A parallel job writes accumulated damage into a `NativeArray<float> byTeam`, indexed by team id. The safety system refuses it — parallel writes to arbitrary indices. Someone adds:

    [NativeDisableParallelForRestriction] public NativeArray<float> ByTeam;

It compiles, it runs, and the totals are right in every test. Under load, once every few minutes, a team's total is short by exactly one hit.
